<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lakshmi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lakshmi is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess of wealth, fortune and prosperity, and the consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. She is often shown seated or standing on a lotus, sometimes flanked by elephants pouring water over her, a form known as Gaja Lakshmi. Her image appears on early Indian coins, from the Kunindas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> to the Ghurid sultan Mu'izz al-Din, and she is especially worshipped at the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakshmi.jpg" alt="Lakshmi" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raja Ravi Varma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma,_Goddess_Lakshmi,_1896.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Lakshmi, also called Shri, does not appear as a fully personified goddess in the earliest Vedic texts. There the word shri names a set of auspicious qualities: radiance, beauty, abundance, power and majesty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, dated to about 1500 BCE, mentions Shri around 130 times, always in connection with prosperity, health, fortune and radiance, and uses the name Lakshmi once, for a mark of auspicious fortune. Scholars such as Mandakranta Bose and David Kinsley see in her the gradual personification of ideals of well-being and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, a pattern found in other Indo-European traditions. Some scholars propose that Shri and Lakshmi were originally different goddesses who merged into one figure.</p><p>In the Vajasaneyi Samhita of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a>, Shri and Lakshmi are named as the two wives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Her importance grew in the late epic period, around 400 CE, when she became closely tied to Vishnu as his consort. In the epics <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> is regarded as an incarnation of Lakshmi, as are Rukmini in the tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and, in the Critical Edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Lakshmi is revered as the goddess of fortune, wealth, prosperity, beauty, fertility and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and with Parvati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a> she forms the Tridevi. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> she is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>'s consort and his divine energy, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a> devotion to her is held to be essential for reaching him. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a> she is the prosperity aspect of the supreme goddess.</p><p>Indian art shows her as an elegantly dressed golden woman seated or standing on a lotus, holding a lotus. Her four arms stand for the four aims of human life: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, artha, kama and moksha. She is often shown with two elephants pouring water over her, the Gaja Lakshmi form, which symbolises fertility and royal authority. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, she rose from the Ocean of Milk when the gods and demons churned it to obtain the nectar of immortality, holding a lotus, and is therefore also called Padma.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> worship Lakshmi at Deepavali, the festival of lights held in autumn, when they light lamps in and outside the home and offer family prayers to her. Other days sacred to her include Lakshmi Panchami in the first week of the Hindu new year, Sharad Purnima and the Lakshmi Puja of Navaratri. Her image is among those most often found at archaeological sites: terracotta figurines from the 3rd century BCE come from Vaisali, Sravasti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kausambi</a>, and she appears on coins of Azes II, Azilises and the Shunga king Jyesthamitra. She is also honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, and in Chinese Buddhism as Gongdetian, the goddess of fortune and prosperity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lakshmi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal religion was Hindu, the brick sanctuaries of Isanapura centred on lingas, with Harihara, Brahma, Indra and <b>Lakshmi</b> also worshipped and Buddhism tolerated beside them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In northern India the Ghurid sultan Mu'izz al-Din Muhammad issued gold coins bearing the image of the Hindu goddess <b>Lakshmi</b>, following local coin designs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The major traditions are devoted to Vishnu, who descends to earth in incarnations (avatars) such as Rama and Krishna; to Shiva, the ascetic and the lord of creation and destruction; and to the Goddess, Devi, in forms such as Durga, Kali, <b>Lakshmi</b> and Parvati.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The later stanzas identify the mother with Hindu goddesses, Durga and <b>Lakshmi</b> among them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li><li><blockquote>The silver coins of King Amoghabhuti, modelled on Indo-Greek drachms, carry legends in both Brahmi and Kharoshthi with a deer and a goddess, possibly <b>Lakshmi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuninda-kingdom">Kuninda Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
